            "text": "Have you ever wondered why we get to build snowmen in the winter, but have to wear swimsuits and splash in the pool during the summer? \n\nA lot of people think it’s because the Earth gets closer to the Sun in the summer and farther away in the winter. But guess what? That’s actually a myth! \n\nThe real secret to our seasons is that **the Earth is tilted!**\n\nImagine the Earth is like a spinning top, but instead of standing straight up and down, it leans a little bit to the side. As the Earth spins, it also takes a giant, year-long trip all the way around the Sun. \n\nBecause the Earth is tilted, different parts of our planet lean toward the Sun at different times of the year:\n\n* **Summer:** When your part of the Earth is leaning *toward* the Sun, the sunlight hits you straight-on, like a flashlight shining directly on your face. The days are longer, the sunlight is extra strong, and everything heats up!\n* **Winter:** Six months later, as the Earth travels to the other side of the Sun, your part is now tilted *away* from the Sun. The sunlight hits at a slant, spreading out its warmth, and the days get shorter. That’s why it turns cold and snowy!\n* **Spring and Fall:** These are the in-between seasons, when the Earth isn't tilted toward or away from the Sun, giving us nice, mild weather.\n\nSo, the next time you jump into a pile of autumn leaves or build a winter snowman, you can thank our planet's special little tilt!\n\nP.S. Did you know that because of the tilt, when it is freezing cold winter in the United States, it is actually hot, sunny summer in Australia? The kids down there celebrate Christmas on the beach!",
